 # Changelog
 
+## [3.58.0] — 2026-11-04 — grounded five-section intent confirmation
+
+- Code-only intent confirmation uses five ordered, localized sections: background and current direction, current problem and impact, what changes, scope and acceptance, and confirmation. Connect known project or function context to the request, with brief evidence-backed sections; broader direction and impact appear only when evidenced and needed, without filling unsupported benefit claims. Preserve supplied facts, qualifiers and outcome strength before drafting, leave unknown facts unresolved, and distinguish rule ownership from deterministic selection or results. The existing single stop, publication authorization and outcome-only decision boundary remain unchanged.
+- Load a short primary content template before intake drafting or replies, with optional relevant illustrations and parity coverage for both portable file pairs. Separate private preparation and reply records from the actual user-message composer; missing required facts stay in the existing interview. State each live-case fact once, preserving its abstraction, present affected population and supplied boundaries.
+- Load the primary template directly from the main code-only entry before both new-intent drafting and open-intent confirmation, retaining the already-confirmed fast path. The no-intent reader loads its rules before writing; restore the original numbered consent-policy anchors without changing their routing or private/user-message separation.
+- Apply six private questions to the actual proposed reply at the existing composer, revising unsupported context or scope, unclear wording or consent, premature structure, unchecked diagrams and unnecessary repetition or private inventory while retaining required disclosures. Explicitly condition both non-forced branch upload and ready-for-review pull request opening on completed review and publication checks; opt-out remains available anytime before publication and merge stays separate.
+- Illustrate the structure with concrete invoice and CSV requests, preserving missing-fact interviews. Use titled ASCII comparisons when helpful, marking expected results and reusing optional visualization tooling with a readable standalone fallback.
+- Preserve existing publication holds until explicitly lifted; bind the full public reply to the five-section skeleton, ground its opening in available project context, and invoke the optional visualization procedure before generating and checking diagrams. Keep unavailable-fact interviews focused and preserve filtered-result qualifiers.
+- Read the available overview and request-relevant evidence at intake entry before interviews or choice replies; use documented facts instead of treating them as unanswered questions, retaining source-scoped uncertainty.
+- Load the five-section layout only for confirmation-ready requests, keeping early grounding/interviews separate. Draft and explain constraints at the source's abstraction, retaining target nouns and qualifiers rather than inferring internal-method restrictions or broader absence/data-preservation claims. Keep the unavailable-date interview in its required grounding reader; keep complete ready invoice/CSV illustrations in the existing optional gallery, linked from the concise ready layout. Stop missing-fact interviews before confirmation preparation, and load the existing titled-flow template before generating a comparison. Explain literally without analogies or unnecessary new terminology.
+- Route incomplete intake through the interview before loading ready confirmation instructions; preserve missing required content in Open questions across both intent templates. Confirmation cannot replace evidence for an unknown date, and absence of evidence establishes neither equality with today nor a different date. Ground the saved Problem and public reply in the same inspected state and supported effects: a missing date is distinct from an incorrect calendar value, and neither establishes announcement history or guaranteed reader understanding.
+
